# CA Certificate Trust — OS Level Only

## Policy

CA certificate trust for all cluster connections (Thanos, OIDC, Kubernetes API) is handled
**exclusively at the OS level** via `update-ca-trust`. No application code may load, bundle,
override, or configure CA certificates.

## What is permitted

- The pod entrypoint script (`run-with-ca-update.sh`) calls `update-ca-trust extract` before the
  JVM starts. This is the one and only place where CA trust is configured.
- The Deployment projected volume in `openshift.yml` mounts three ConfigMaps into
  `/etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/`.
- The `Dockerfile.jvm` grants group-write permission to `/etc/pki/ca-trust/` so the non-root
  process can run `update-ca-trust extract`.

## What is forbidden

- **No** `TlsVerificationSupport` class or equivalent — it has been deleted.
- **No** `quarkus.tls.*` or `quarkus.oidc.tls.*` configuration properties emitted from any
  config source factory.
- **No** declared **`quarkus-tls-registry` artifact** in this project’s `pom.xml` (do not add it
  just to configure trust stores). The Quarkus platform may still pull that extension **transitively**
  unused — that is acceptable. What matters is **no PEM/trust-store TLS configuration** in app
  code or config (`quarkus.tls.*`, custom `SSLContext`).
- **No** `SSLContext` construction from PEM files in application code.
- **No** `ignoreCerts`, `clusterCaBundlePath`, `tlsVerificationEnabled` config properties in
  `Reson8Config` or any other config mapping.
- **No** per-client trust store overrides of any kind in application properties, YAML, or
  ConfigMaps.
- **No** configuration overrides that allow bypassing the CA trust requirement.

## Required CA sources

Three ConfigMaps are projected into the anchors directory. Their availability is enforced in
`run-with-ca-update.sh` with hard failures and no override path:

| Anchors file | Source ConfigMap | Availability |
|---|---|---|
| `service-ca.crt` | `openshift-service-ca.crt` | Auto-created by OpenShift in every namespace — 100% available; if absent, the pod must fail. |
| `ingress-ca.crt` | `reson8-trusted-ca-bundle` key `ca-bundle.crt` | Injected by `cluster-network-operator` after the one-time cluster-admin setup in `docs/reference/platform/cluster-ca-setup.md`. The application assumes this has been done; it fails hard if the file is missing. |
| `kube-ca.crt` | `kube-root-ca.crt` | Auto-created by OpenShift in every namespace. |

## Ingress/router CA — one-time cluster-admin operation (assumed done)

The router CA for `*.apps.*` (required for OIDC) does not inject automatically. A cluster admin
must run the three-step setup documented in `docs/reference/platform/cluster-ca-setup.md`. The application
**assumes this has been done** and fails on startup if `ingress-ca.crt` is absent.

Do **not** suggest workarounds (disabling TLS, mounting the cert manually, adding config
overrides) if the ingress CA is missing. Fail hard and direct the operator to `docs/reference/platform/cluster-ca-setup.md`.

## Anti-patterns

```java
// ❌ Application-level PEM loading — deleted, never reintroduce
TlsVerificationSupport.sslContext(true, Optional.empty());

// ❌ Per-client custom SSLContext
SSLContext ssl = TlsVerificationSupport.sslContext(false, config.k8s().thanos().clusterCaBundlePath());
HttpClient.newBuilder().sslContext(ssl).build();

// ❌ Quarkus TLS registry cert configuration
props.put("quarkus.tls.reson8-oidc.trust-store.pem.certs", "/etc/reson8/ca/ca-bundle.crt");
```

```java
// ✅ JVM default trust — correct after update-ca-trust ran in entrypoint
HttpClient.newBuilder().connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5)).build();
```

## If a new connection type needs HTTPS

Use `HttpClient.newBuilder().build()` (JVM default trust). All cluster CAs are already in the
trust store. Do not add cert configuration — if a connection fails due to TLS, the fix is in the
entrypoint/projected-volume/cluster-admin setup, not in application code.
